\description{
Draws from the posterior predictive distribution: the family's
observation noise around the parameters of each posterior draw.
They predict new observations, for the training data or new data.
\code{\link[=pp_check.mvgam]{pp_check.mvgam()}} draws them under the fitted state to check a fit
against its data.
}
\details{
Each family draws from its own distribution at the parameters
\code{\link[=posterior_linpred.mvgam]{posterior_linpred.mvgam()}} and \code{\link[=posterior_epred.mvgam]{posterior_epred.mvgam()}} use. A
\code{trunc()} term restricts the draws to its bounds.

\code{\link[=hindcast.mvgam]{hindcast.mvgam()}} draws under the fitted latent states at the
training times. \code{\link[=forecast.mvgam]{forecast.mvgam()}} propagates them to future times.
}
\section{Trend state}{

By default the trend enters through its covariate part, without the
state it took at each observed time. The \pkg{brms} counterparts of
this method use that fitted state. \code{incl_autocor = TRUE} uses it
here too, as \code{\link[=fitted.mvgam]{fitted.mvgam()}} and \code{\link[=pp_check.mvgam]{pp_check.mvgam()}} do on the
training data. The section 'Which prediction function?' of
\code{\link[=predict.mvgam]{predict.mvgam()}} compares the prediction functions.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
set.seed(13)
simdat <- sim_mvgam(family = poisson(), n_series = 1L,
                    n_timepoints = 120L, trend_model = AR())
mod <- mvgam(y ~ s(x, k = 5), trend_formula = ~ AR(p = 1),
             data = simdat$data_train, family = poisson(),
             chains = 2, silent = 2)

# Simulated counts, one row per draw and one column per observation
pp <- posterior_predict(mod)
dim(pp)

# The share of observed counts inside their 90 percent predictive
# intervals
bounds <- apply(pp, 2, quantile, probs = c(0.05, 0.95))
y <- simdat$data_train$y
mean(y >= bounds[1, ] & y <= bounds[2, ])

# Next: pp_check() plots the draws against the observed counts
}

}
